Directives modify an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their containing template. Source directives attach to the next eligible top-level declaration or dependency clause.

### Template-head directives

Put a template directive in the head of the template it modifies:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]
]

`$md` formats this template's body, not its parent's. Other template directives control slots, contributions and formatting. See @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).

### Source directives

Source directives occupy their own line and each has a specific target.

`$export` publishes a declaration or a direct-selection dependency clause in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth`:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    greeting #= "Hello"

    $export
    @helpers render_greeting
]

`$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding in `config.moth` or a selected source module as a build input:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

The name is the input key, the declared type is its contract and the initializer is its fallback. Only a `$config` binding may omit an initializer. See @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values).

### Attachment

A source directive attaches to the next eligible item with no blank physical line between them. Comment-only lines are allowed:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    -- Public name for other modules.
    page_title #= "Welcome"
]

An empty or whitespace-only line breaks attachment. A directive cannot skip an unrelated item, cross a scope boundary or reach past the end of the file. LF and CRLF line endings obey the same rule. At most one source modifier may attach to an item.

`$export` and `$config` take no operands. `$export` cannot modify a namespace dependency, ordinary runtime statement or `$config` binding. `$config` cannot modify a dependency clause. Neither directive is valid in a template head.

### Directive operands

The compiler defines each directive's supported operands. A directive may take a bare compile-time value or one named value:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    $name
    $name value
    $name key = value
]

A named value has one key and value, without a type annotation. Parenthesized calls such as `$name(...)` are not directive syntax. For example, `$slot title` declares a slot, `$slot title = "Untitled"` gives it a default and `$code "moth"` selects syntax highlighting.

Template-head directives are `$before`, `$after`, `$fresh`, `$slot`, `$in`, `$note`, `$todo`, `$doc`, `$raw`, `$md`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code`. Source directives are `$export` and `$config`.

### Context and ownership

The compiler rejects unknown names, unexpected operands, duplicate source modifiers, missing targets and directives used in the wrong context. An invalid target is an error even if the directive name is known.

Module roles are assigned by `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth` filenames, not directives. Project and builder settings are ordinary records in `config.moth`. Moth programs and builders cannot register new directives or use them as macros.

See @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API), @../project-structure/#module-roots (Module roots) and @../project-structure/#project-config (Project configuration).

